$OctTwoArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'reverse-printed-window-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Reverse-Printed Window Stickers: Readable From Outside'
    Description = 'Plan inside-glass window stickers around reverse printing, white ink, viewing direction, glass cleaning and removal before ordering.'
    Category = 'Window graphics'
    CardDescription = 'Keep the message facing the street while the adhesive sits safely on the inside of the glass.'
    Alt = 'Photographic storefront window with an original OPEN TODAY reverse-printed sticker and botanical illustration visible from outside'
    Caption = 'The street-side view, not the flat artwork file, decides whether a reverse print works.'
    Published = '2026-10-02'
    Faq = @(
      @('Which side of the window receives a reverse-printed sticker?', 'The adhesive usually goes on the inside so the artwork reads correctly from outside. Confirm the glass construction and the intended viewing side in the proof.'),
      @('Is white ink always needed on a clear window sticker?', 'No, but transparent colors can lose contrast against a changing background. Proof a white backing option against the actual window and lighting.'),
      @('Can an inside-glass sticker be removed cleanly?', 'Removal depends on adhesive, glass surface, dwell time and cleaning method. Trial a sample on the real pane and agree on the removal process.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A shop owner may love a window graphic on a white PDF and wonder why it disappears on the street. Behind the glass are shelves, reflections and a bright afternoon sky. The message needs to survive all three. I would start with a photograph taken from the pavement, at the distance where a passerby first sees the window.</p>
  <p>An inside-glass sticker is usually printed in reverse so the adhesive faces the glass and the artwork reads normally from outside. That sounds simple until the proof is mirrored the wrong way, the white backing covers an area meant to remain clear, or a door handle hides the opening hours. Mark the street side and application side directly on the proof.</p>
  <h2>Map the Actual Viewing Conditions</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>What to send</th><th>What to proof</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Where will it sit?</td><td>Photo of the exact pane with dimensions</td><td>Handle, frame and eye-level position</td></tr><tr><td>Who reads it?</td><td>Street-side viewing photograph</td><td>Legibility at normal walking distance</td></tr><tr><td>What sits behind it?</td><td>Day and evening background photos</td><td>Color and white-ink contrast</td></tr><tr><td>How long will it stay?</td><td>Campaign dates and removal plan</td><td>Suitable sample and cleaning method</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Keep the first message short. An original leaf illustration and clear OPEN TODAY lettering might work for a small cafe; a dense menu will not become easier to read because it is printed sharply. Use the smallest realistic size, not a magnified screen view, to check thin strokes and letter spacing.</p>
  <h2>Decide Where White Ink Belongs</h2>
  <p>On clear film, some colored areas remain translucent. A white underprint can give important words and illustrations a solid base while leaving other areas transparent. Ask for a proof that marks the white layer separately. The same artwork can look different against a dark shop interior, a pale display or backlighting after sunset.</p>
  <p>Do not describe every window as suitable for the same construction. Tinted, coated, textured or treated glass can change the result, and a film may not be appropriate for some glazing. The installer or glass owner should confirm compatibility. Test the proposed construction on a representative pane before committing to the full run.</p>
  <h2>Make Installation and Removal Part of the Order</h2>
  <p>Measure the clean, usable glass rather than the full frame opening. Decide whether staff will hand-apply a few pieces or an installer will place a larger graphic. Ask for an application method that matches the material. If the campaign is temporary, agree on who removes it and how the pane will be cleaned afterward.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Mark inside and outside on every proof</li><li>Send exact pane dimensions and street-side photos</li><li>Identify handles, frames and any sightline obstruction</li><li>Proof white ink and small text at final size</li><li>Trial adhesion and removal on the real glass</li><li>Inspect the installed sample in daylight and after dark</li></ul>
  <p>Compare <a href="/blog/clear-stickers-vs-white-vinyl-stickers/">clear film with white vinyl</a> before choosing opacity, and use the <a href="/blog/white-ink-small-text-custom-stickers/">white ink guide</a> when fine type matters. Send your window photographs and intended campaign duration through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. A good window sticker starts with the view from the other side.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'easy-peel-tab-stickers-guide'
    Title = 'Easy-Peel Tab Stickers for Busy Packing Benches'
    Description = 'Specify easy-peel sticker tabs with attention to liner access, tab shape, application speed, usable print area and packing format.'
    Category = 'Application workflow'
    CardDescription = 'Make the first peel predictable without sacrificing the printed message or finished-pack appearance.'
    Alt = 'Close-up photograph of a hand lifting a small pull tab from an original illustrated bakery seal sticker on its liner'
    Caption = 'A peel tab is useful only when the packer can find it without studying the liner.'
    Published = '2026-10-02'
    Faq = @(
      @('Does an easy-peel tab change the finished sticker shape?', 'It may. A non-adhesive or extended tab can remain visible after application unless the design includes a removable handling area. Proof the applied result.'),
      @('Can every sticker be supplied with a peel tab?', 'Tab feasibility depends on dimensions, die-cut geometry, liner and production method. Share the actual size and request a physical sample.'),
      @('Is a split-back liner the same as a peel tab?', 'No. A split backing can help expose adhesive, while a tab gives the finger a grasp point. Compare both in the real packing workflow.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>At a quiet desk, almost any sticker peels. At a packing bench, one worker may be closing dozens of boxes and reaching for a seal with the other hand. A tiny corner that takes three attempts matters more than a decorative flourish. I would watch one full packing cycle before choosing the tab.</p>
  <p>Easy-peel can mean different constructions: a protruding corner, a dry lift area, a split-back liner or a kiss-cut shape with extra backing around it. They do not produce the same finished look. The buyer needs to decide whether a tab stays visible on the pack, folds under, or is only part of the liner that gets thrown away.</p>
  <h2>Test the Peel in the Real Sequence</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>Potential advantage</th><th>Check before quoting</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Extended liner margin</td><td>Room to flex the backing</td><td>Extra space in storage and packing</td></tr><tr><td>Split-back liner</td><td>Adhesive can be exposed in stages</td><td>Whether the split is easy to find</td></tr><tr><td>Dry lift tab</td><td>Finger can grasp a non-stick area</td><td>Finished-pack appearance</td></tr><tr><td>Kiss-cut sheet</td><td>Backing stays around the design</td><td>Spacing and waste on each sheet</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Give a sample to the person who actually seals the package. Have them wear the gloves used in normal production, if any. Time and observe a short run without turning it into an unsupported productivity claim. Does the tab help, or does it catch on neighboring labels? Does the second hand still need to steady the sheet?</p>
  <h2>Protect the Artwork and the Seal</h2>
  <p>The pull area must not remove important brand text or weaken the part meant to close the package. For a bakery box, an original wheat illustration and clear product name can sit in the main seal while a small corner gives the finger somewhere to start. Proof the sticker both on its liner and applied across the box flap. If the package surface is rough or dusty, test adhesion separately from peel ease.</p>
  <p>Do not assume a larger tab is always better. It can turn a tidy seal into a loose flag that customers catch on a bag. If the tab is only on the liner, say so in the dieline and packing instructions. A drawing that shows only the printed face can hide the exact handling problem the project is meant to solve.</p>
  <h2>Send a Usable Quote Brief</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a photo or short description of the packing motion</li><li>State whether stickers arrive on rolls, sheets or loose</li><li>Measure the finished seal and the possible pull area</li><li>Identify gloves, speed and one-handed handling needs</li><li>Proof the applied pack as well as the liner</li><li>Test tab access and adhesion with actual packaging</li></ul>
  <p>Compare <a href="/blog/roll-labels-vs-sheet-stickers/">rolls and sheets</a> for the application flow, and review the <a href="/blog/sticker-sheets-vs-die-cut-stickers/">kiss-cut versus die-cut guide</a> for backing choices. Put the pack photo, dimensions and proposed tab position into the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A small handling detail can determine whether the label is pleasant to use.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'label-liner-choice-guide'
    Title = 'Label Liner Choice: Paper or Film for Your Workflow?'
    Description = 'Compare paper and film release liners for custom labels by application method, die-cutting, stability, waste and trial requirements.'
    Category = 'Label construction'
    CardDescription = 'The discarded backing still affects dispensing, hand peeling and the quality of the finished label.'
    Alt = 'Photographic workbench comparison of illustrated label rolls on translucent film and cream paper release liners'
    Caption = 'A liner disappears after application, but it does important work before then.'
    Published = '2026-10-02'
    Faq = @(
      @('Is a film liner always better for automatic application?', 'No. Machine compatibility depends on the applicator, sensors, label size and release characteristics. Trial the exact construction on the intended line.'),
      @('Can paper and film liners be recycled in the same stream?', 'Do not assume so. Collection options depend on local facilities and the liner construction. Ask the waste handler before making a recyclability claim.'),
      @('Does liner choice change the label face material?', 'They are separate layers, but the combination affects dispensing and die-cutting. Approve the complete construction rather than choosing each layer in isolation.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A buyer can spend an hour comparing matte and gloss, then write “any backing” in the order notes. The backing is thrown away, so it feels unimportant. At an applicator, however, the liner carries every label past a sensor, around a peel plate and into the waste roll. I would ask where the label is applied before settling the liner.</p>
  <p>Paper liners, including glassine-type options, and film liners can differ in thickness, tear behavior, transparency, moisture response and release. None of those properties makes one material universally superior. The useful question is whether the proposed full construction works with your label geometry and application method.</p>
  <h2>Start With the Applicator, Not a Material Name</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Application</th><th>What the liner must do</th><th>Trial to request</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hand peel from sheet</td><td>Release without tearing or curling</td><td>Peel after normal storage</td></tr><tr><td>Hand dispense from roll</td><td>Wind and unwind cleanly</td><td>Short run with real dispenser</td></tr><tr><td>Automatic labeling</td><td>Track, sense and strip consistently</td><td>Trial on the named machine</td></tr><tr><td>Small complex die-cut</td><td>Support cuts without liner damage</td><td>Inspect waste removal and edge quality</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Ask the line operator for the machine model, sensor type, dispensing direction, roll dimensions and current failure mode. A transparent film liner may behave differently with optical sensors; a paper liner may respond differently to humidity or bending. These are reasons to test, not grounds for a blanket promise.</p>
  <h2>Check the Cut and the Waste</h2>
  <p>Very small labels or pointed shapes leave little room for error. If the die cuts too deep, the liner may split; too shallow and labels can remain connected to the waste matrix. Inspect a physical sample after die-cutting, not just the digital cutline. If operators must strip the waste, watch whether small shapes lift accidentally.</p>
  <p>Also ask where spent liner goes. It may be collected at the applicator, mixed with general waste or sent to a specialist program. Avoid describing either paper or film as recyclable without a real collection route and material confirmation. The buyer's waste process is part of the specification.</p>
  <h2>Approve the Whole Construction</h2>
  <p>Send the exact label face, adhesive, size and application surface together with the liner request. A trial roll should match the intended quantity per roll, core, winding and storage conditions as closely as possible. Run it long enough to observe tracking and dispensing, then check the applied label. A smooth unwind is not success if the label lands crooked or lifts later.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Document manual or machine application</li><li>Provide applicator and sensor information</li><li>Confirm label dimensions and cut complexity</li><li>Inspect die-cut depth and matrix stripping</li><li>Trial the proposed liner after normal storage</li><li>Verify the actual local waste route before making claims</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/label-roll-unwind-direction-core-size-guide/">roll direction and core guide</a> for machine setup, and the <a href="/blog/roll-labels-vs-sheet-stickers/">roll versus sheet guide</a> for format selection. Send machine details and a sample of your current label through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The backing deserves attention precisely because the customer will never see it.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'thermal-transfer-printable-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Thermal-Transfer Printable Labels: Plan the Overprint'
    Description = 'Plan preprinted labels for thermal-transfer overprinting around printer fit, ribbon, variable fields, scannability and proof approval.'
    Category = 'Variable-data labels'
    CardDescription = 'Leave a reliable blank field for lot, date or serial data that will be printed later.'
    Alt = 'Photographic desktop thermal-transfer printer feeding original illustrated olive oil labels with clear variable lot and date fields'
    Caption = 'The decorative print and the later variable-data print must work as one label.'
    Published = '2026-10-02'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can a thermal-transfer printer print over any preprinted label?', 'No. Face material, coating, ribbon and printer settings must be compatible. Test the exact combination at production size.'),
      @('Should variable fields be printed by the label factory or the packer?', 'Either workflow can be considered. Decide who owns the data, when it becomes final and which printer performs the overprint before approving artwork.'),
      @('Is a barcode readable if it looks sharp to the eye?', 'Not necessarily. Print and scan a sample with the actual data length, ribbon and scanner after application to the real package.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A preprinted bottle label can be ready months before the fill date is known. The brand wants its illustration, color and product story in place; the packer needs to add a lot number or date at the last responsible moment. I would treat that second print pass as part of the label design, not an afterthought squeezed into the remaining white corner.</p>
  <p>Thermal-transfer overprinting uses a ribbon and a compatible print surface. A material that accepts beautiful factory artwork may not accept the packer's ribbon cleanly. The printer, ribbon grade, face stock, coating and field size have to be tried together. No generic “thermal-transfer compatible” description replaces a sample on the actual setup.</p>
  <h2>Reserve the Variable Zone Before Designing the Front</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Field</th><th>Owner question</th><th>Proof check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lot code</td><td>Who generates and validates it?</td><td>Longest realistic code fits</td></tr><tr><td>Date</td><td>Which format and market wording?</td><td>Correct human-readable copy</td></tr><tr><td>Barcode or 2D code</td><td>What data and scanner?</td><td>Printed sample scans after application</td></tr><tr><td>Blank area</td><td>Where will the ribbon print?</td><td>No artwork or varnish interference</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Give the variable area generous margins. If the label wraps around a bottle, a field near the curve can be harder to read or scan. Use the actual package photo to decide whether the data belongs on the back label, a side panel or a separate small label. The responsible brand or packer must approve any required date and product information.</p>
  <h2>Run Both Print Passes on a Real Sample</h2>
  <p>Request a short batch of the proposed preprinted label. Feed it through the intended thermal-transfer printer with the ribbon and settings the packer plans to use. Check smearing, missing dots, contrast, registration and abrasion from normal handling. Then apply a sample to the filled or representative package and scan any code there.</p>
  <p>For an illustrated olive oil label, the decorative branch motif can live around the variable panel, but it must not intrude into the lot and date area. A white box is not a design failure when it has a clear operational purpose. The difference from a blank placeholder is that its size, location and future content are specified and tested.</p>
  <h2>Control the File and Reprint Routine</h2>
  <p>Decide who loads the data file, who checks the first label after a ribbon change and what happens to misprints. If labels are preprinted in several product variants, keep the roll identity visible so a correct lot code is not put onto the wrong artwork. A good approval record includes the fixed artwork version and the variable-data template version.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide printer model, resolution and ribbon details</li><li>Reserve a measured variable-data field</li><li>Use the longest expected data in the proof</li><li>Overprint and scan physical samples</li><li>Check applied labels on the final package</li><li>Define first-label inspection and misprint handling</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/qr-code-stickers-scan-testing-guide/">code testing guide</a> covers scanning, while the <a href="/blog/custom-olive-oil-bottle-labels-guide/">olive oil label guide</a> covers bottle layout. Upload your label sketch and printer details through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. Designing for the second print pass is how a good-looking label stays useful on packing day.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'custom-egg-carton-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Custom Egg Carton Labels: Fit the Lid and the Data'
    Description = 'Plan custom egg carton labels around molded-fiber lids, hinges, handling, readable product identity and owner-approved pack information.'
    Category = 'Food packaging labels'
    CardDescription = 'Give the carton a recognizable front without covering its hinge, closure or changing pack information.'
    Alt = 'Photographic molded-fiber egg carton with an original illustrated hen and sun label beside an open carton of eggs'
    Caption = 'The lid shape and closure decide where an egg carton label can actually work.'
    Published = '2026-10-02'
    Faq = @(
      @('Will one label size fit every egg carton?', 'Not reliably. Lid geometry, molded texture, capacity and closure vary. Measure and test the exact carton before approving a dieline.'),
      @('Can the label carry egg grade or origin claims?', 'Only with wording and evidence supplied and approved by the responsible food business for its target market. The printer should not invent those claims.'),
      @('Is a wraparound label better than a top label?', 'It depends on the carton shape and opening method. Apply physical samples of both and confirm that the carton still opens, closes and stacks normally.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>An egg carton looks flat from above, but its molded lid has shallow hills, recesses and a hinge that moves. A rectangular label can bridge the high points and lift at the low ones. I would put the actual empty carton on the table, open and close it, then draw the usable label area with low-tack tape before discussing the illustration.</p>
  <p>For a six-pack or twelve-pack, the best label position may differ. Some cartons have a broad top panel; others need a shorter front strip. A charming original hen and sunrise drawing can make the pack recognizable, but the product name and approved details still need room at a readable size.</p>
  <h2>Measure the Carton in Three States</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>State</th><th>What changes</th><th>Label check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Empty and closed</td><td>Lid contour is visible</td><td>Contact area and edge position</td></tr><tr><td>Filled and closed</td><td>Carton may flex</td><td>Adhesion and readable front</td></tr><tr><td>Open</td><td>Hinge and latch move</td><td>No tearing or blocked access</td></tr><tr><td>Stacked</td><td>Pressure and rubbing increase</td><td>Scuffing and label transfer</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Molded fiber is not a single uniform surface. Dust, texture and recycled-content variation can affect contact. Test a proposed adhesive and face material on a production carton after its usual storage and handling. Do not promise a particular hold from an image of a smooth paper sample.</p>
  <h2>Keep Variable and Required Information Under Control</h2>
  <p>The food business, not the sticker supplier, owns grade, origin, packing date, best-before and other market-specific statements. Decide which information is fixed in the artwork and which changes by batch. A secondary field or overprint zone may be more practical than reprinting the entire illustrated label whenever a date changes.</p>
  <p>Make the primary identity easy to see from a refrigerated shelf or market crate. Check the final-size proof under ordinary lighting. Decorative lettering that works on a poster may become illegible on a narrow carton strip. If a seal crosses the opening, confirm whether it is simply decorative or intended as a closure indication; do not imply tamper evidence without an appropriate construction and trial.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Whole Packed Carton</h2>
  <p>Apply a small run in the same sequence the packer will use. Open, close, stack and transport representative cartons. Look for edge lift at the lid curve and for labels that catch on adjacent packs. If the cartons are chilled, include that condition in the sample test. The attractive flat proof is only the first checkpoint.</p>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send photos and dimensions of the exact carton</li><li>Mark hinge, latch and molded high points</li><li>Supply owner-approved product copy and variable-data plan</li><li>Proof small type at actual size</li><li>Apply samples to filled cartons</li><li>Open, stack and handle the labeled cartons</li></ul>
  <p>Compare the <a href="/blog/custom-bakery-box-stickers-guide/">bakery box label guide</a> for another paper-pack format and the <a href="/blog/thermal-transfer-printable-labels-guide/">variable-data overprint guide</a> for later lot or date printing. Share the real carton and approved copy through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The lid has the last word on the label shape.</p>
'@
  }
)
